WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.950
We spent the last pages on what ACID means.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.901
Now watch it turn into code.

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:07.851
Every transactional database gives you the same three words.

00:00:07.851 --> 00:00:09.802
Start.

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:11.752
Make permanent.

00:00:11.752 --> 00:00:13.703
Undo.

00:00:13.703 --> 00:00:15.653
An entire field hides behind them.

00:00:15.653 --> 00:00:19.659
Here are the three.

00:00:19.659 --> 00:00:23.664
The first marks the start of a transaction.

00:00:23.664 --> 00:00:27.669
The second makes every change since the start permanent and visible to everyone else.

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:31.675
The third throws all of it away, as if nothing happened.

00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:35.680
Rolling back like that is also called aborting the transaction, the word the rest of this module uses.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.686
Outside that block, every statement stands alone.

00:00:39.686 --> 00:00:43.691
Each one commits by itself.

00:00:43.691 --> 00:00:47.697
Inside the block, many statements become one logical operation, no matter how many there are.

00:00:47.697 --> 00:00:52.140
Each example shows the happy path, the statements that run when the purchase succeeds.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.582
If a check fails, not enough stock or money, the app rolls back every write since the start.

00:00:56.582 --> 00:01:01.025
A broken constraint does the same.

00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:03.601
Example one.

00:01:03.601 --> 00:01:06.177
Atomicity.

00:01:06.177 --> 00:01:08.753
Taylor Swift tickets drop at five p m.

00:01:08.753 --> 00:01:11.328
Alice clicks buy two tickets, and that one click touches two tables.

00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:13.904
Walk the block top to bottom.

00:01:13.904 --> 00:01:16.480
We begin the transaction.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.056
A select reads the open seats.

00:01:19.056 --> 00:01:21.632
An update reserves two of them.

00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:24.208
An insert writes Alice's order.

00:01:24.208 --> 00:01:26.784
Then commit makes all of it permanent at once.

00:01:26.784 --> 00:01:29.360
Now suppose that insert fails halfway.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.935
A crash.

00:01:31.935 --> 00:01:34.511
A full disk.

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:37.087
Every change since begin rolls back, and the seats un-reserve.

00:01:37.087 --> 00:01:39.663
The purchase happens end to end, or it never happened at all.

00:01:39.663 --> 00:01:42.058
Example two.

00:01:42.058 --> 00:01:44.453
Consistency.

00:01:44.453 --> 00:01:46.848
Alice sends a hundred dollars to Bob.

00:01:46.848 --> 00:01:49.243
Walk the block.

00:01:49.243 --> 00:01:51.638
We begin the transaction.

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:54.033
An update debits Alice a hundred.

00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:56.428
A second update credits Bob the same hundred.

00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:58.823
An insert records the transfer.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:01.218
Then commit.

00:02:01.218 --> 00:02:03.613
But there is a guard.

00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:06.008
The Accounts table carries a rule.

00:02:06.008 --> 00:02:08.403
No balance may go below zero.

00:02:08.403 --> 00:02:10.798
Say Alice has only fifty dollars and tries to send a hundred.

00:02:10.798 --> 00:02:13.193
The database rejects the debit.

00:02:13.193 --> 00:02:15.588
The rule fires, the whole transaction aborts, and Bob's credit rolls back with it.

00:02:15.588 --> 00:02:17.983
The balance can never go red.

00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:20.549
Example three.

00:02:20.549 --> 00:02:23.115
Isolation.

00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:25.680
Black Friday, midnight.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.246
One Labubu left.

00:02:28.246 --> 00:02:30.812
A thousand customers tap buy at the same instant.

00:02:30.812 --> 00:02:33.378
Without isolation, all thousand grab the last unit at once.

00:02:33.378 --> 00:02:35.944
Isolation lines them up.

00:02:35.944 --> 00:02:38.510
Walk one winner's block.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.075
We begin the transaction.

00:02:41.075 --> 00:02:43.641
A select reads the quantity, and sees one.

00:02:43.641 --> 00:02:46.207
An update decrements it to zero.

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:48.773
An insert records the order.

00:02:48.773 --> 00:02:51.339
Then commit.

00:02:51.339 --> 00:02:53.904
The database locked that Labubu row, so the other nine hundred ninety nine wait.

00:02:53.904 --> 00:02:56.470
By the time they get in, the quantity reads zero, and they roll back.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.036
Exactly one Labubu walks out the door.

00:02:59.036 --> 00:03:01.563
Example four.

00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:04.090
Isolation again, sharper.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.617
A celebrity posts, and ten thousand likes land in five seconds.

00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:09.144
Two things must stay in sync.

00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:11.671
The Likes table, one row per like.

00:03:11.671 --> 00:03:14.198
And a single cached total on the post.

00:03:14.198 --> 00:03:16.725
Walk one like's block.

00:03:16.725 --> 00:03:19.252
We begin the transaction.

00:03:19.252 --> 00:03:21.779
An insert records the like.

00:03:21.779 --> 00:03:24.306
An update increments the counter by one.

00:03:24.306 --> 00:03:26.834
Then commit.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:29.361
Without the wrapper, two likes both read the total at five hundred.

00:03:29.361 --> 00:03:31.888
Both write five hundred one.

00:03:31.888 --> 00:03:34.415
Two likes landed, so where is the counter?

00:03:34.415 --> 00:03:36.942
Five hundred one.

00:03:36.942 --> 00:03:39.469
One like vanished.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:41.996
The lost update.

00:03:41.996 --> 00:03:44.523
With the wrapper, the update locks the counter row, and likes queue.

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:47.050
Each reads, adds one, writes back.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.577
Ten thousand likes.

00:03:49.577 --> 00:03:52.104
Ten thousand increments.

00:03:52.104 --> 00:03:54.631
Every time.

00:03:54.631 --> 00:03:58.351
Four scenarios, one move.

00:03:58.351 --> 00:04:02.072
Wrap your statements between start and make permanent whenever you touch more than one row or table.

00:04:02.072 --> 00:04:05.792
Inside that block, your reads and writes see one consistent world, and the database serializes everyone else around you.

00:04:05.792 --> 00:04:09.512
You write the business logic.

00:04:09.512 --> 00:04:13.233
The database keeps it correct.

00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:16.953
So you know how to write one transaction.

00:04:16.953 --> 00:04:20.673
The deeper question comes when thousands of them interleave on the same rows.

00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:24.394
What makes that result correct?

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:28.114
That is what the next section works out, starting with a quick graph refresher.
